        en: `## The author and the book

Mikhail Bulgakov (1891–1940), born in Kiev, a doctor by training, wrote «Белая гвардия» (1925), the satires «Роковые яйца» and «Собачье сердце» (1925, unpublished until 1987), and plays that were banned one after another. He began «Мастер и Маргарита» in 1928, burned the first draft in 1930, wrote it again and dictated the last corrections blind, in 1940, weeks before his death. His widow Elena Sergeyevna kept the manuscript; the novel appeared, cut, in the journal «Москва» in 1966–1967 and in full in 1973.

## Three layers

| Layer | Time and place | Main figures | Tone |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| **Moscow** | four days in May, 1930s | Woland and the retinue: Koroviev-Fagot, the cat Behemoth, Azazello, Hella; the editor Berlioz, the poet Ivan Bezdomny, the theatre men Likhodeyev, Rimsky, Varenukha; the writers of MASSOLIT | satire, farce, the fantastic |
| **The Master and Margarita** | the same Moscow, told in flashback from the clinic | the nameless Master, author of a novel about Pilate; Margarita Nikolaevna; the critic Latunsky; the neighbour Aloisy Mogarych | lyric, tragic |
| **Yershalaim** | one day, the 14th of Nisan | Pontius Pilate, the prisoner Yeshua Ha-Nozri, Levi Matvei, Judas of Kiriath, Afranius, the centurion Mark Ratslayer, the dog Banga | the historical novel, grave and exact |

The Yershalaim chapters (2, 16, 25, 26) are the Master's novel, and they enter the book in three ways: Woland tells the first at the Patriarch's Ponds, Ivan dreams the second, Margarita reads the rest from the restored manuscript. A **novel within the novel**: the Master's fate repeats Yeshua's, Berlioz's atheism is answered by Woland's story, and the last chapter brings the two worlds together on the moonlit road where Pilate is released.

## Woland and the epigraph

The epigraph is from Goethe's «Faust»: «Я — часть той силы, что вечно хочет зла и вечно совершает благо». Woland is the devil who punishes rather than tempts: the Variety theatre audience grabs the raining money and the free dresses and is exposed; Berlioz loses his head under a tram after denying that anything is above him; the informers, bribetakers and literary hacks get what they have earned. Satan's ball is the judgement of the Moscow of denunciations. Woland does no evil of his own: Moscow supplies it, and he makes it visible. That is the sense in which he «совершает благо».

## Pilate and Yeshua

The Yershalaim chapters remove the miracles and leave two men. Yeshua, a wandering philosopher with one disciple who writes down what he never said, insists that «злых людей нет на свете» and that every power is violence over people. Pilate, with a headache, a dog and a hatred of the city, knows he is innocent, tries to save him and signs the sentence out of fear of Caesar. His punishment is two thousand years of insomnia on a stone chair, and the sentence he hears in a dream is the novel's moral centre: «трусость — самый тяжкий порок». At the end the Master releases his hero with one phrase, «Свободен!», and Pilate walks up the moonlit road to finish the conversation.

## The Master and Margarita

The Master wrote the truth, was destroyed by critics for it, burned the book and went mad. Margarita, married and unhappy, sells her soul to Woland to save him: she becomes a witch, smashes Latunsky's flat, is hostess at the ball and asks, as her reward, first for Frida, a stranger, and only then for the Master. Woland restores the burned novel with the sentence that became proverbial: «Рукописи не горят». But the Master is not given light: Levi Matvei brings the verdict that he «не заслужил света, он заслужил покой». He did not fight to the end for his book, and peace, the quiet house with the Venetian window, is the mercy for a tired man. Ivan Bezdomny becomes Professor Ponyrev and dreams every spring full moon of the road and the two walkers.

### Exercises

1. Name the three layers of the novel and one character from each.
2. How do the Yershalaim chapters enter the Moscow story?
3. What is the epigraph, and how does Woland fulfil it?
4. Why is Berlioz punished?
5. What does Pilate call the heaviest vice, and why does the sentence concern himself?
6. What does Margarita ask for first at the ball, and what does the choice show?
7. Why does the Master receive peace rather than light?

### Answers

Moscow with Woland, Berlioz and Ivan; the love story with the Master, Margarita and Latunsky; Yershalaim with Pilate, Yeshua and Levi Matvei. Woland tells the first Pilate chapter at the Patriarch's Ponds, Ivan dreams the second in the clinic, and Margarita reads the rest from the manuscript Woland restores. The epigraph from Faust says the devil wants evil and does good; Woland punishes the greedy, the liars and the informers so that their evil becomes visible, and so does good without intending it. Berlioz denies that there is any power above him, and the proof is given at once: his head under the tram, exactly as Woland foretold. He calls cowardice the heaviest vice; he himself sent an innocent man to death out of fear of Caesar, so the sentence is his own verdict. She asks for Frida to be freed from her handkerchief, a stranger she promised to help, and only then for the Master: her mercy comes before her love. He gave up the fight for his book and his truth, so he has not earned light, but he has earned rest, and the quiet house is the mercy given to him.`,
