        en: `## Alexander Tvardovsky (1910–1971)

Born near Smolensk, the son of a village blacksmith who was dekulakised and exiled in 1931 while the son was already a published poet; that wound is the subject of his last poem, «По праву памяти» (1969, printed only in 1987). His first success was «Страна Муравия» (1936), the peasant Nikita Morgunok's search for a land without collective farms. In the war he was a front correspondent and wrote the book that every soldier read.

| Work | Years | What it is |
| --- | --- | --- |
| «Василий Тёркин» | 1942–1945 | «Книга про бойца», thirty chapters printed in front newspapers as the war went |
| «Дом у дороги» | 1946 | the lyric chronicle of a peasant family driven into Germany |
| «Я убит подо Ржевом» | 1946 | the monologue of a dead soldier |
| «За далью — даль» | 1950–1960 | the travel poem across Siberia with the chapter «Так это было» about Stalin |
| «По праву памяти» | 1969 | the confession about his father and the «сын за отца не отвечает» of 1935 |

## «Василий Тёркин»: a book without beginning or end

Tvardovsky says in the chapter «От автора» that the book is «без начала, без конца», because in war a reader may die before the next chapter: each chapter must stand alone. The hero is an ordinary infantryman from Smolensk, «обыкновенный», not a Hero of the Soviet Union, and his name was chosen for the verb тереть, to rub: «тёртый», worn smooth by life. He is the collective soldier: in every unit there is such a man, «В каждой роте есть всегда, / Да и в каждом взводе».

| Chapter | Content | Note |
| --- | --- | --- |
| «Переправа» | the crossing of a river under fire; Tyorkin swims back through November water to report that the platoon is alive | the refrain «Переправа, переправа! / Берег левый, берег правый» and the dead who «уже не услышат» |
| «О награде» | Tyorkin wants no order, only a medal, to show the girls at home | humour as the soldier's weapon |
| «Гармонь» | he plays the dead tank-commander's accordion and the frozen soldiers dance | the accordion passes from the dead to the living |
| «Поединок» | hand-to-hand with a German, «как на древнем поле боя» | the epic duel reduced to a fist fight |
| «Два солдата» | Tyorkin fixes an old man's saw and clock and promises «побьём, отец» | the soldier and the peasant are one people |
| «Смерть и воин» | wounded in the snow, he argues with Death and refuses to go | the folk tale of the soldier who outwits death |

The book's metre is the four-foot trochee of Russian folk song and of Pushkin's tales, and its language is the soldiers' own: «Бой идёт святой и правый, / Смертный бой не ради славы, / Ради жизни на земле». There is no commissar, no Stalin and almost no commander in the whole book, which was noticed and forgiven because the soldiers loved it.

## «Я убит подо Ржевом» (1946)

A soldier killed in the summer of 1942 «в безымянном болоте» speaks to the living: he does not know whether Rzhev was taken, whether the Don was held, whether Berlin is ours. He asks for nothing except that they live and remember: «Я вам жизнь завещаю, — / Что я больше могу?». The monologue of the dead is a device Tvardovsky uses to make the victory belong to those who did not see it. «Я знаю, никакой моей вины» (1966), eight lines, is the survivor's answer: no guilt, «но всё же, всё же, всё же…».

## Tvardovsky the editor

As editor of «Новый мир» (1950–1954 and 1958–1970) Tvardovsky made the journal the centre of honest literature of the thaw: he printed Solzhenitsyn's «Один день Ивана Денисовича» in 1962, having obtained Khrushchev's permission personally, and the lieutenant prose, Bykov and Baklanov. He was removed in 1970 and died a year later.

## Zabolotsky: the late simplicity

Nikolai Zabolotsky (1903–1958) began in the 1920s with the grotesque «Столбцы», spent 1938–1944 in the camps, and returned to a classical, simple lyric. «Некрасивая девочка» (1955) watches a plain child run in the yard and ends with the question every exam quotes: «что есть красота… / Сосуд она, в котором пустота, / Или огонь, мерцающий в сосуде?». «Не позволяй душе лениться» (1958) is an imperative poem of eight stanzas: «Душа обязана трудиться / И день и ночь, и день и ночь!». Between Tvardovsky's soldier and Zabolotsky's soul lies the whole range of the lyric allowed after the war.

### Exercises

1. Why is «Василий Тёркин» a book «без начала, без конца»?
2. Explain the name Tyorkin.
3. What does Tyorkin do in «Переправа», and what is the refrain?
4. What is the folk source of «Смерть и воин»?
5. Who speaks in «Я убит подо Ржевом», and what does he ask?
6. What did Tvardovsky publish in 1962 and how?
7. What question ends «Некрасивая девочка»?

### Answers

Because a soldier reading it in a front newspaper might not live to the next issue, each chapter had to be complete in itself. The name comes from тёртый, a man rubbed smooth by life, an ordinary experienced soldier. He swims back across the icy river under fire to report that the platoon on the far bank is alive, and the refrain is «Переправа, переправа! Берег левый, берег правый». The folk tale of the soldier who argues with Death and tricks or outlasts her. A soldier killed near Rzhev in 1942 speaks to the living, asks whether the war was won, and bequeaths them his life, asking only that they live and remember. He published Solzhenitsyn's «Один день Ивана Денисовича» in «Новый мир», after obtaining Khrushchev's personal permission. Whether beauty is a vessel with emptiness inside or a fire flickering in the vessel.`,
